WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.501 --> 00:00:02.176
- [Narrator] Open up your
wallet and you'd expect to find

00:00:02.176 --> 00:00:05.039
ones, 10s, 20s, and maybe even a hundo.

00:00:05.039 --> 00:00:07.706
The last thing you'd ever expect is a Tom,

00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:10.150
also known as a $2 bill.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.067
Here's why:

00:00:12.431 --> 00:00:15.209
♫ Let me show you the money

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:17.964
It was 1862, the federal
government had just started

00:00:17.964 --> 00:00:19.798
printing its first paper money.

00:00:19.798 --> 00:00:23.807
The first and only denominations
were the $1 and $2 bills.

00:00:23.807 --> 00:00:25.372
And keep in mind, at the time,

00:00:25.372 --> 00:00:27.695
people were making about $15 a month.

00:00:27.695 --> 00:00:30.412
To pay using a $2 bill was a status symbol

00:00:30.412 --> 00:00:32.788
on the wealthiest of
Americans could afford,

00:00:32.788 --> 00:00:34.491
and a lot of the time they used them

00:00:34.491 --> 00:00:37.284
for under the table transactions
like political bribes,

00:00:37.284 --> 00:00:39.451
prostitutes, and gambling.

00:00:41.745 --> 00:00:45.469
The Tom ultimately got a bad
rap and was deemed unnecessary.

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:48.207
In 1966, the government
took a 10-year hiatus

00:00:48.207 --> 00:00:51.656
and stopped printing those bad boys.

00:00:51.656 --> 00:00:55.153
But in 1976, the government
gave them another try,

00:00:55.153 --> 00:00:56.738
this time classing them up a bit,

00:00:56.738 --> 00:00:59.656
issuing a special bicentennial bill.

00:00:59.656 --> 00:01:02.301
A little too classy, some would argue.

00:01:02.301 --> 00:01:04.572
People began hiding them
away as collectors items,

00:01:04.572 --> 00:01:06.693
leading to less bills in circulation,

00:01:06.693 --> 00:01:08.657
which brings us to present day.

00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:10.706
Though rare, Toms are still being printed

00:01:10.706 --> 00:01:13.356
and there are over a
billion in circulation.

00:01:13.356 --> 00:01:14.940
Most people keep them as keepsakes,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.705
though there is a
sub-culture of $2 ambassadors

00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:20.051
who are set on keeping the
Tom alive by using them

00:01:20.051 --> 00:01:21.576
every chance they get.

00:01:21.576 --> 00:01:23.198
So, the next time you're paying with cash,

00:01:23.198 --> 00:01:24.865
go ahead, use a Tom.

